@user, agreed, that would be even better. My suggested solution was born from the idea of having minimal changes in streamlit to get an 80% solution. 

Can you elaborate more on your suggestion to give the streamlit developers a hint on how you would approach this?

What I understood is that `my-streamlit-dashboard` would register a streamlit `entry_point` which `streamlit` would then find and just provide. It could then be just listed when running `streamlit ls` (for showing all found dashboards) and run with `streamlit show my-streamlit-dashboard`, for instance to have a differentiation to `streamlit run your_script.py`.

@user The idea of creating another GitHub account has been brought up repeatedly in this thread. That's a pretty dissatisfying workaround as well - it centers around giving Streamlit access to all that account's private repos. The workaround I suggested avoids giving Streamlit private repo access at all.

I can't even see a reason they should be asking for write access to public repos. There are plenty of public repos that are important in supply-chain issues. If all they need is the code, I don't see any need for two-way access. What am I missing? I do understand the need to write keys on private repos.

Edit to add that I've been using Streamlit for the last couple weeks, and thinking about exactly what permissions it needs. For a public project deployed to Community Cloud, Streamlit sets a push web hook on the repository. That requires read access for the repository, but it doesn't require write access. I still can't think of any reason Streamlit would need write access to public repositories.

